t {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[2x]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =(4-0)\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560101 1080 "56" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560101 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560101 1083 #uuid "c14d9583-f641-5ba8-a269-37856b6aa647" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560102 1080 "114" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560102 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560102 1083 #uuid "c20f5bf6-6c83-5812-875c-16020afddb1d" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560103 1080 "100" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560103 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560103 1083 #uuid "c384aa9d-923b-599e-bc01-7f1f75575e83" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560119 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560104 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1$, $x=3$, $y=1$, $y=3$, $z=2$, $z=4$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={e}^{z}\\,i+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+4{x}^{2}yz\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560104 1072 #uuid "c6fa7469-cadf-5bb3-8dec-3f881cb31af1" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560104 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2 x^{2} y+4 x^{2} y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=1}^{x=3}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=3}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =(27-1)\\cdot (9-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =416.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560105 1080 "-190" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560105 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560105 1083 #uuid "c8b23a3e-7c17-526a-928a-85fad0743c35" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560106 1080 "14" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560106 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560106 1083 #uuid "ca848204-1be2-5069-9e32-ed8d35b33b43" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560107 1080 "12" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560107 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560107 1083 #uuid "d296efa3-d724-5a7c-9971-af1e70d02d75" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560108 1080 "-114" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560108 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560108 1083 #uuid "d8ca3ef4-5b6a-5f8d-b1e9-486e15852860" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560109 1080 "\\frac{76}{3}" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560109 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560109 1083 #uuid "db3cd37d-26c8-565b-a403-fb51d1549af5" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [17592186560110 1080 "-416" 13194139536036 17592186045443 true] [1759